00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.171
sniper, robotic explorer,

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.206
as it did

00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:08.074
touch down on the lunar surface

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:09.275
in the last hour.

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.244
They'll provide updates just in a bit.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:13.213
Let's get to CNN's Christine Fisher.

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:16.082
It appears the moon sniper did land,

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:18.284
but not in the Sea of Tranquility

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:19.919
somewhere else.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:21.187
Correct?

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.256
It's called the Sea of nectar.

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:24.991
Sara And what we know

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:26.993
is that this spacecraft called Slim,

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:28.728
which is short for the Smart Lander

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:30.597
for investigating the moon.

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.165
It has touched down

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:34.000
on the surface of the moon.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.602
But the big question is,

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:37.504
what kind of condition is it in?

00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:39.272
The spacecraft is communicating

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:40.840
with the ground specifically.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.076
Japan's space agency, or JAXA.

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:46.246
But did the spacecraft perhaps

00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:49.582
tip over rollover or worst case scenario,

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:51.384
did it completely crash

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:52.919
into the surface of the moon?

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:54.554
We just don't know yet.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:55.789
And we're awaiting

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:57.023
a press conference

00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:59.359
from JAXA, the Japanese space agency

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.294
Any minute now we're hopefully

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:02.462
we will get some answers.

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.664
And so it's really too soon to say,

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:05.398
excuse me

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:07.801
if this was a success or a failure,

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.169
because you know what

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:10.270
What is a success

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:11.304
if you land on the moon,

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:12.806
that is a pretty big feat

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:14.040
in and of itself.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.775
But then if you tip over

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:16.709
and you can't perform

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:18.211
some of the scientific observations

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:19.846
that you were hoping for it to perform.

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:21.681
Does that still constitute a success?

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:23.349
So a lot of questions here.

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:25.351
But Sarah, if successful,

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:29.055
this will be such a huge deal for Japan.

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:30.390
And it space agency

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.758
because it will become

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:33.426
only the fifth country

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:34.928
in the history of the world

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:35.795
to land on

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:37.931
the surface of the moon successfully

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:39.699
and only the third country

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:40.433
this century,

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:43.970
only China and India have done it.

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:46.339
It's been more than 50 years

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:47.373
since the U.S.

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:47.941
has done

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.909
it, which was back in 1972

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:51.678
at the end of the Apollo program.

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:53.346
So really a momentous moment

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:54.914
for Japan Space Agency.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:56.816
Everybody is really holding their breath,

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:59.252
waiting to see what is going to happen.

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:00.253
And, you know, in addition

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:03.256
to these historic firsts, potentially

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.993
this rover called that the moon sniper

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:08.495
is demonstrating this really cool

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:09.796
pinpoint technology

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:11.464
trying to land in an area

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:13.500
just the size of a football field.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.335
You compare that to previous landers.

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:16.102
Their target

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:18.271
landing site was several kilometers

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:19.539
or even miles long.

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:21.207
So if successful,

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:23.409
not just a historic first for Japan,

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:25.178
but also a really impressive

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:25.778
step forward

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:28.081
in terms of the types of technology

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.416
for these kinds of lunar landers.

